//? Title: Vanilla saliency:visible MNIST Adam and signed raw-logit derivatives
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An image classifier assigns a category,but its local response to individual pixels remains hidden. Train a small MNIST CNN with every tensor Adam update visible,then inspect signed raw-logit derivatives with fixed targets and initialization controls on genuine held-out digits.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.11_interpretability/vanilla_saliency.cpp`.

> The method

For fixed class c,the first-order response is s_c(x+delta)-s_c(x) approximately inner_product(gradient_x s_c,delta). Signed coordinates distinguish local increases from decreases. The gradient is computed for the raw categorical score,not a probability or a fitting loss.

$$ g_i=\frac{\partial}{\partial x_i}\sum_b s_{c_b}(x_b). $$
\alttext(Differentiate the sum of fixed selected raw logits with respect to the input pixels;independent batch rows retain their individual derivative scale.)

A two-pixel fixture makes sign and batch scaling hand-checkable. The shared CNN uses genuine MNIST observations,explicit tensor Adam and validation-only selection;fresh restoration precedes every held-out metric and explanation map.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/vanilla_saliency_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/vanilla_saliency_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/vanilla_saliency_concept.tikz > build/vanilla_saliency_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/vanilla_saliency_concept.svg build/vanilla_saliency_concept.dvi > build/vanilla_saliency_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
